Myth: Before the night sky knew the glimmer of stars, the world was suffocated by the Gale-Wyvern, a titanic beast of black thunderheads whose massive wings choked the heavens in endless, roaring storms. Coveting the sky's warmth, the Wyvern snatched the exhausted Sun from the horizon, dragging it into the swirling eye of a perpetual hurricane. While the great eagles fell to the torrential despair, a tiny, resilient wind-beetle named Lumis refused to yield.

Flying upward through miles of crushing, violent winds, Lumis discovered the Sun trapped within the Wyvern’s vortex of lightning. "Great Sun," Lumis clicked, his tiny voice piercing the thunder, "I will free your light."

Mustering its final strength, the fading Sun sparked a single, blinding ember of pure lightning. Lumis swallowed the radiant spark. Instantly, his dark shell ignited with a searing, pulsing electricity, illuminating the tempest.

Enraged by the sudden brightness, the Gale-Wyvern snapped its jagged beak to consume the invader. Lumis darted forward, a blazing comet of golden thunder, and plunged directly into the beast’s maw, releasing the concentrated storm-fire. The colossal explosion shattered the hurricane beast. Its gleaming silver beak became the moon, its shattered lightning scales formed the stars, and the falling golden sparks became fireflies, tiny guardians keeping the dark at bay.
